**Read:** [Introduction to Historical Geology — Estrada and contributing authors](../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD), the preservation, reworking, gaps, and extinction/history material; [International Stratigraphic Guide, abridged version — ICS](../sources/International-Stratigraphic-Guide.lingua#r_6NAYJE2KFXGRNCWP6TEY7AZE7J), unconformity-bounded units and relationships; [Introduction to Geochronology — Pieter Vermeesch](../sources/Introduction-to-Geochronology-Vermeesch.lingua#r_30AEAXYK3VV89FDX1TF9ZKJ84J), the introduction and basic notions as preparation.

The geological record is produced, modified, removed, and sampled through processes. Erosion, nondeposition, dissolution, metamorphism, deformation, weathering, transport, and preservation can alter what remains observable. A missing interval is evidence to investigate rather than an empty place to fill with an unconstrained story.

Taphonomic reasoning examines how biological remains enter and change within the record. Distinguish an original community, a death assemblage, a transported concentration, and the sampled fossil association. Time averaging and reworking can combine evidence from different events. Similar preservation biases can affect comparisons of diversity or extinction through time.

Unconformities and other discontinuities require a field/section account: what surfaces and relationships are observed, what interval/event is inferred, and what remains unresolved. A present erosional surface and an ancient surface preserved in a stratigraphic sequence need different contextual observations.

**Work:** compare two historical interpretations of a deliberately incomplete hypothetical section. Explain how preservation or sampling can mimic a change in environment or biological abundance. Identify an observation that would distinguish a true event from a record-production effect, and preserve the uncertainty when it cannot yet be resolved.

Read the complete historical examples and relevant guide sections. Follow [Distinguish nuclear change from ordinary chemical transformation](../../../../hephaestus/chemistry/general-chemistry/descriptive/Nuclides-decay-and-nuclear-change.lingua#r_0Y7ZKCD61XN1RHC4HKMBDZPK35) for the shared nuclide/decay/measurement foundation before numerical dating. Its geology continuation returns to the age equation, attaching physical calculations to the particular event and preservation conditions they can constrain.

